          "kind": "informational",
          "level": "none",
          "guid": "mt9jwq0nvnag1wfk",
          "message": {
            "text": "The picker's \"AA\" marker is derived from each theme's own colors (isThemeWcagAA), computed at the /themes route (here (0)) — never a hand-maintained boolean. This is deliberate: a curated flag would drift the moment someone tweaks a palette, and the marker would then lie about compliance. Deriving it means the badge is always true to the actual contrast.\n\nThe audited pair set (primary/muted text on both bg levels, links, line numbers, the four file-status labels on their tints) is the definition of \"AA\" for a theme here. tests/unit/themes/theme-contrast.test.ts pins the exact resulting set — Dark, Light, High Contrast ×2 — so a future edit that drops a labeled theme below AA, or lifts a brand theme into it, fails the test and forces a conscious update rather than silently changing what the UI advertises.\n\nBrand-replica themes (Dracula, Solarized, Nord, …) copy their namesake palettes, which aren't AA; they're intentionally left unmarked rather than de-branded to pass.\n",
            "markdown": "The picker's \"AA\" marker is **derived** from each theme's own colors (`isThemeWcagAA`), computed at the `/themes` route ([here](0)) — never a hand-maintained boolean. This is deliberate: a curated flag would drift the moment someone tweaks a palette, and the marker would then lie about compliance. Deriving it means the badge is always true to the actual contrast.\n\nThe audited pair set (primary/muted text on both bg levels, links, line numbers, the four file-status labels on their tints) is the definition of \"AA\" for a theme here. `tests/unit/themes/theme-contrast.test.ts` pins the exact resulting set — Dark, Light, High Contrast ×2 — so a future edit that drops a labeled theme below AA, or lifts a brand theme into it, fails the test and forces a conscious update rather than silently changing what the UI advertises.\n\nBrand-replica themes (Dracula, Solarized, Nord, …) copy their namesake palettes, which aren't AA; they're intentionally left unmarked rather than de-branded to pass.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/themes/contrast.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 62,
                  "endLine": 72,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    { label: 'text on surface', fg: c.text, bg: c['bg-surface'] },\n    { label: 'muted text on bg', fg: c['text-dim'], bg: c.bg },\n    { label: 'muted text on surface', fg: c['text-dim'], bg: c['bg-surface'] },\n    { label: 'link/accent on bg', fg: c.accent, bg: c.bg },\n    { label: 'line numbers', fg: c['gutter-text'], bg: c['gutter-bg'] },\n  ];\n  for (const [status, [pastel, token]] of Object.entries(STATUS_TINT)) {\n    pairs.push({ label: `status: ${status}`, fg: c[token], bg: compositeOver(pastel, 0.1, c['bg-surface']) });\n  }\n  return pairs.map((p) => ({ label: p.label, ratio: contrastRatio(p.fg, p.bg) }));\n}"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
